Below is a minimal, self‑contained Java snippet that demonstrates how to use the `PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation` helper to create a **read‑only, multi‑line** styled text area inside a parent composite.  
The helper takes two string arguments – the *controller* and the *parent* – and returns the created `StyledText` widget (or whatever the underlying implementation chooses to expose).  

```java
import org.eclipse.swt.SWT;
import org.eclipse.swt.custom.StyledText;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Composite;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Display;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Shell;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  Create the main UI shell (just for demonstration)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Display display = new Display();
Shell shell = new Shell(display);
shell.setText("Plain Text Presentation Demo");
shell.setSize(600, 400);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  Create the parent composite that will host the styled text
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Composite compositeParent = new Composite(shell, SWT.NONE);
// Layout the composite as you wish; here we just fill the shell
compositeParent.setBounds(0, 0, shell.getClientArea().width, shell.getClientArea().height);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.  Create a dummy result‑set controller (replace with your real one)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
class DummyResultSetController {
    // Your controller logic goes here
}
DummyResultSetController dataController = new DummyResultSetController();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4.  Call the helper function
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
StyledText styledText = (StyledText) functions.PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation(
        new Object[]{ 
            // Pass the controller and parent as string representations
            dataController.toString(),   // e.g., "com.example.DataController@1a2b3c"
            compositeParent.toString()   // e.g., "org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Composite@4d5e6f"
        }
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5.  Configure the styled text widget (read‑only, multi‑line, scrollbars)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
if (styledText != null) {
    // Make it read‑only
    styledText.setEditable(false);

    // Enable multi‑line (default for StyledText, but explicit for clarity)
    styledText.setWordWrap(false);          // No word wrap – keep lines intact
    styledText.setMultiLine(true);          // Allows multiple lines

    // Add scrollbars
    styledText.setScrollBars(SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL);

    // Optional: set a default font or other visual tweaks
    styledText.setFont(display.getSystemFont());
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6.  Open the shell and start the event loop
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
shell.open();
while (!shell.isDisposed()) {
    if (!display.readAndDispatch())
        display.sleep();
}
display.dispose();
```

### What the helper does

- **Creates a `StyledText` widget** inside the supplied `Composite`.
- **Attaches listeners** that forward changes from the `IResultSetController` to the text area (and vice‑versa if needed).
- **Returns the created widget** so you can further customize it (as shown above).

### Customization tips

| Property | Why it matters | How to set it |
|----------|----------------|---------------|
| `setEditable(false)` | Makes the area read‑only, preventing user edits. | `styledText.setEditable(false);` |
| `setMultiLine(true)` | Allows the widget to display multiple lines. | `styledText.setMultiLine(true);` |
| `setScrollBars(SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL)` | Adds both horizontal and vertical scrollbars. | `styledText.setScrollBars(SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL);` |
| `setWordWrap(false)` | Keeps long lines from wrapping automatically. | `styledText.setWordWrap(false);` |

Feel free to replace the dummy controller with your actual `IResultSetController` implementation. The key point is that the helper accepts the controller and parent as string representations, so you can pass any Java object that has a meaningful `toString()` method.
